# Compiling Starviewer

Starviewer is linked against its own patched builds of DCMTK, VTK, GDCM and
ITK (`sdk/patches/`). The patches change behaviour, not just compilation
(for example, GDCM's error handling so that corrupt or truncated files are
reported instead of being displayed silently wrong). Distribution packages of
these libraries do not carry the patches: do not build Starviewer against
them, even when it happens to compile.

Qt and ThreadWeaver are not patched and can come from system packages. On
Windows, Qt 5.15.2 `msvc2019_64` from the Qt online installer or
`aqtinstall` works.

The CI workflows (`.github/workflows/linux.yml` and `windows.yml`) run the
same steps described below and are the reference for how to build and package
Starviewer on a clean machine.

## Building on Linux (verified on Ubuntu 24.04, GCC 13.3)

System packages:

```sh
sudo apt-get install build-essential cmake git rsync wget xsltproc \
  libssl-dev zlib1g-dev libgl1-mesa-dev libglu1-mesa-dev libxt-dev \
  qtbase5-dev qttools5-dev qtdeclarative5-dev libqt5xmlpatterns5-dev \
  libqt5opengl5-dev libqt5sql5-sqlite \
  qml-module-qtquick2 qml-module-qtquick-controls qml-module-qtquick-controls2 \
  qml-module-qtquick-layouts qml-module-qtquick-window2 qml-module-qtqml-models2 \
  libkf5threadweaver-dev extra-cmake-modules \
  qtbase5-gtk-platformtheme qttranslations5-l10n qtwayland5 \
  xvfb xauth
```

(`qtbase5-gtk-platformtheme`, `qttranslations5-l10n` and `qtwayland5` are
only needed to build the packages.)

Patched SDK (downloads are checksum-verified; takes over an hour):

```sh
export SDK_BASE_PREFIX=$HOME/starviewer-sdk QTDIR=/usr LIBS="dcmtk vtk gdcm itk"
cd sdk/scripts/unix
./sdk_download.sh && ./sdk_extract.sh > /dev/null && ./sdk_build.sh
```

Starviewer:

```sh
cmake -S starviewer -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=RelWithDebInfo \
  -DSDK_INSTALL_DIR=$SDK_BASE_PREFIX/sdk-install -DQT_DIR=/usr \
  -DBUILD_PDF_MANUALS=OFF
cmake --build build --parallel
```

Tests:

```sh
cd build
LD_LIBRARY_PATH=$SDK_BASE_PREFIX/sdk-install/lib \
  xvfb-run -a -s "-screen 0 1280x1024x24" ctest --output-on-failure
```

## Building on Windows (verified in CI: Windows Server 2022, MSVC 19.44)

`.github/workflows/windows.yml` is the reference. It uses Visual Studio 2022
(x64), Qt 5.15.2 `msvc2019_64`, MSYS2 (the `MSYS` environment with
`path-type: inherit`, so MSVC, Qt and CMake stay on `PATH`) and jom. The SDK
scripts run in that MSYS2 shell, built `RelWithDebInfo`, and include
ThreadWeaver and ECM (`LIBS="dcmtk vtk gdcm itk ecm threadweaver"`).

Things that matter when reproducing it by hand:

- Keep `SDK_BASE_PREFIX` short (CI uses `D:/sdk`): VTK and ITK exceed the
  Windows path length limit otherwise.
- Give the SDK scripts the POSIX form of that path (`cygpath -u`). MSYS2's tar
  reads `D:/...` as a remote host.
- Remove or shadow MSYS2's `/usr/bin/link.exe`, which hides MSVC's linker.
- Configure Starviewer with `-DSDK_INSTALL_RELEASE_DIR=<SDK install>` (not
  `SDK_INSTALL_DIR`, which is only an alias on MSVC) and with jom on `PATH`.
- Run the tests with `sdk-install/bin` and Qt's `bin` on `PATH`.

## Packaging

The packages bundle Qt (libraries, plugins, translations), ICU, and the SDK
shared libraries (GDCM and vtkgdcm), so the only system dependencies are the
low-level ones listed in `CPACK_DEBIAN_PACKAGE_DEPENDS`
(`starviewer/CMake/Installers.cmake`). DCMTK, VTK and ITK are linked static;
on Linux ThreadWeaver comes from a system package.

Package builds must be configured with `-DUSE_QTCONF=ON`, which embeds a
`qt.conf` in the resources so that Qt looks for plugins and translations next
to the installed executable instead of in the development Qt. Do not run the
tests on a `USE_QTCONF=ON` build: from a build tree, the embedded qt.conf
hides the development Qt plugins. Configure and build once with
`USE_QTCONF=OFF` to test, then reconfigure with `-DUSE_QTCONF=ON` and
rebuild (only the main executable is relinked) before packaging.

On Linux, from the build directory:

```sh
cpack -G DEB                                       # starviewer~<version>~<arch>-<os>.deb
../.github/scripts/make-appimage.sh .              # starviewer~<version>~<arch>.AppImage
```

On Windows, install [NSIS](https://nsis.sourceforge.io) and make sure
`makensis.exe` is on `PATH`, then from the build directory:

```sh
cpack -G NSIS                                      # starviewer~<version>~<arch>-winnt.exe
```

The `*-winnt` installer bundles the MSVC runtime libraries. It registers the
`starviewer://` URL protocol.

The PDF manuals are not part of the packages unless they were built
(`BUILD_PDF_MANUALS=ON`, which needs LibreOffice); when they are built, the
installers also create shortcuts to them and the Linux package installs a
"How to access IFU" menu entry.
